<title>UK: UK retail sales rise 0.1 pct in April.</title>
<headline>UK retail sales rise 0.1 pct in April.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-05-22</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Office for National Statistics released the following seasonally adjusted data on UK retail sales in April.</p>
<p>    RETAIL SALES VOLUME    APRIL	 MARCH     (PREL MAR)</p>
<p>    Monthly s/adj change    0.1	  0.7	 (0.3)</p>
<p>    Yr-on-yr change	   4.7	  4.4	 (4.0)</p>
<p>    Index (base 1990=100)  114.3	 114.2     (113.8)</p>
<p>    3m/3m			 1.2	  1.1	 (1.0)</p>
<p>    3m/3m yr ago		4.5	  4.6	 (4.4)</p>
<p>Market forecasts had centred on a 0.3 percent rise in retail sales on the month and a year-on-year increase of 4.1 percent.  </p>
<p>BY SECTOR (percentage gain, minus indicates fall)</p>
<p>			     m/m	y/y	3m/3m    3m/3m yr ago</p>
<p> Predominantly food   -0.1	2.5	0.5	   2.6</p>
<p> total pred non-food   0.2	6.5	1.8	   6.5</p>
<p> non-specialised	-0.7	2.2     -0.4	   4.8</p>
<p> Textile,clothing,foot-0.1	7.4	1.8	   6.7</p>
<p> Household goods	 0.5	9.6	2.3	   9.1</p>
<p> Other stores	    0.5	4.4	2.4	   4.6</p>
<p>non-store retailing</p>
<p> and repair		0.5	5.4	1.4	   1.9</p>
<p>The average weekly non-seasonally adjusted value of retail sales in April was 3.25 billion stg, up 5.7 percent on April 1996.</p>
<p>In value terms, retail sales rose by 5.5 percent in the three months to end April on the same period a year earlier.</p>
</text>
